WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.800
I've really gotten into piano.

00:00:01.800 --> 00:00:02.793
I just love it.

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:05.130
Especially Beethoven,

00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:06.510
I play Beethoven every night

00:00:06.510 --> 00:00:07.950
on my Discord server.

00:00:07.950 --> 00:00:09.420
Just like 20 people show up,

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:10.830
but I just play through the sonatas

00:00:10.830 --> 00:00:11.663
over and over.

00:00:11.663 --> 00:00:14.820
It just makes me feel incredible.

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:16.470
Doesn't matter what
happened during the day,

00:00:16.470 --> 00:00:17.430
I just feel great.

00:00:17.430 --> 00:00:19.260
And what is it about Beethoven?

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:20.227
I love that I'm acting like

00:00:20.227 --> 00:00:21.660
"this Beethoven guy,"

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:23.520
but specifically,

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:25.110
what is it about his music

00:00:25.110 --> 00:00:26.490
that's getting you right now?

00:00:26.490 --> 00:00:27.810
Oh, I've always loved it.

00:00:27.810 --> 00:00:28.643
Right.

00:00:28.643 --> 00:00:29.476
Since I was a teenager,

00:00:29.476 --> 00:00:31.830
but now I can semi-play it.

00:00:31.830 --> 00:00:33.060
So that's-

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:33.893
Yeah.

00:00:33.893 --> 00:00:35.820
It's just, I don't know.

00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:36.653
He's like-

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:39.120
It's just how my brain works.

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:41.070
He just gets my attention

00:00:41.070 --> 00:00:41.903
and he keeps it

00:00:41.903 --> 00:00:43.710
and takes me to all these different places

00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:46.115
and it's such an emotional ride.

00:00:46.115 --> 00:00:48.210
Put on Mozart and I space out

00:00:48.210 --> 00:00:49.200
after five seconds.

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:50.280
Can't get into it.

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:51.113
Right.

00:00:51.113 --> 00:00:52.844
So, Tim,

00:00:52.844 --> 00:00:54.150
you mentioned that

00:00:54.150 --> 00:00:55.860
now it's about piano for you.

00:00:55.860 --> 00:00:57.510
Are you teaching yourself?

00:00:57.510 --> 00:00:59.220
Do you have a tutor?

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:01.200
No, I don't really-

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:03.540
I just play Beethoven, really.

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:05.250
I'm not learning how to play.

00:01:05.250 --> 00:01:06.120
And then I jam, too.

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:07.530
I know, but you're angering me,

00:01:07.530 --> 00:01:08.498
because-

00:01:08.498 --> 00:01:09.331
(Sona laughs)

00:01:09.331 --> 00:01:10.700
That's enraging me how talented you are.

00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:13.593
I'm sorry, it's just making me mad.

00:01:13.593 --> 00:01:14.673
(Conan laughs)

00:01:14.673 --> 00:01:16.620
I started in Earnest about six years ago.

00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:19.110
So every night I would just start playing.

00:01:19.110 --> 00:01:20.160
And, you know,

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:21.450
at that time it would take me a week

00:01:21.450 --> 00:01:22.740
to get through one Sonata.

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:25.200
And now it's like two days.

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:26.033
I mean-

00:01:26.033 --> 00:01:26.939
Sorry.

00:01:26.939 --> 00:01:27.772
Two evenings-

00:01:27.772 --> 00:01:29.760
Two hours to get through a Sonata.

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:33.300
No, I'm kind of blown away by that.

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:36.330
My relationship, I love playing music,

00:01:36.330 --> 00:01:39.300
I love hacking around on guitar,

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:42.180
but it has never been intuitive for me.

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:43.893
I don't have that, which is,

00:01:44.730 --> 00:01:46.598
and I've always really envied people

00:01:46.598 --> 00:01:50.250
that have that ability to figure it out,

00:01:50.250 --> 00:01:51.270
hear something,

00:01:51.270 --> 00:01:52.650
and then it goes through their brain

00:01:52.650 --> 00:01:54.060
and then they can make it happen

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:54.893
with their hands.

00:01:54.893 --> 00:01:56.670
I need to actually-

00:01:56.670 --> 00:01:57.900
I need to figure out

00:01:57.900 --> 00:01:59.072
what is that shape

00:01:59.072 --> 00:02:01.012
and I need to memorize it.

00:02:01.012 --> 00:02:01.845
Hmm.

00:02:01.845 --> 00:02:03.393
And that's how it works for me.

00:02:04.457 --> 00:02:08.353
And it's why that is not my profession.

00:02:08.353 --> 00:02:10.410
(Sona laughs)

00:02:10.410 --> 00:02:13.620
But I'm really amazed, like,

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:15.330
I'm trying to think of you

00:02:15.330 --> 00:02:18.150
as a kid and you're
listening to classical music,

00:02:18.150 --> 00:02:19.140
but you're also, I'm sure,

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:20.640
listening to-

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:21.473
Heavy metal.

00:02:21.473 --> 00:02:22.440
Heavy Metal.

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:24.697
And at what point do you say,

00:02:24.697 --> 00:02:26.040
"I need to get my own guitar?"

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:28.020
Is that pretty early on?

00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:28.853
Yeah.

00:02:28.853 --> 00:02:31.863
It was last day of eighth grade.

00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:34.680
There was a performance

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:36.510
by some other eighth grader,

00:02:36.510 --> 00:02:37.650
eighth grade boys.

00:02:37.650 --> 00:02:38.557
They went up and did

00:02:38.557 --> 00:02:40.740
"Metal Health" by Quiet Riot

00:02:40.740 --> 00:02:43.260
in front of the whole school.

00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:45.300
And these were kids I knew

00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:46.860
and they were just regular kids

00:02:46.860 --> 00:02:49.530
and yet they were
playing these instruments

00:02:49.530 --> 00:02:50.880
and it just sounded incredible.

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:51.960
It was just magic.

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:52.867
I was like,

00:02:52.867 --> 00:02:54.600
"I should be able to do this."

00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:56.160
And I got a guitar

00:02:56.160 --> 00:02:59.070
and started a cover band,

00:02:59.070 --> 00:03:00.750
did a bunch of metal songs,

00:03:00.750 --> 00:03:04.350
and then moved out to
LA with my metal band,

00:03:04.350 --> 00:03:07.140
and we thought we were
gonna make it like that,

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:08.853
but it didn't pan out and-

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:10.743
Yeah.

00:03:10.743 --> 00:03:11.801
So whatever happened to you?

00:03:11.801 --> 00:03:14.220
(everyone laughs)

00:03:14.220 --> 00:03:15.330
You never made it in music

00:03:15.330 --> 00:03:16.800
and then what happened?

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:17.970
That's how it sounded for a second.

00:03:17.970 --> 00:03:18.803
Sorry.

00:03:18.803 --> 00:03:19.920
Well, getting the job at Tower

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:20.753
really helped me.

00:03:20.753 --> 00:03:21.711
Yeah.

00:03:21.711 --> 00:03:24.990
I got exposed to all
different kinds of music.

00:03:24.990 --> 00:03:27.720
Not just the latest, cool music,

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:29.580
but also going back to the 60s

00:03:29.580 --> 00:03:31.470
and hearing The Beach Boys

00:03:31.470 --> 00:03:32.303
and The Beatles,

00:03:32.303 --> 00:03:35.730
and then talking to other employees there.

00:03:35.730 --> 00:03:36.870
Some of them had been-

00:03:36.870 --> 00:03:38.850
They were kind of lifers at Tower Records

00:03:38.850 --> 00:03:41.340
and they're just so knowledgeable.

00:03:41.340 --> 00:03:42.600
And-

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:43.470
What year is this now?

00:03:43.470 --> 00:03:44.963
This is like early 90s?

00:03:44.963 --> 00:03:46.221
Yeah. 90, 91.

00:03:46.221 --> 00:03:47.054
Yeah.

00:03:47.054 --> 00:03:48.451
And I met met the Weezer guys

00:03:48.451 --> 00:03:50.760
through my coworkers there,

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:52.770
and we formed the band.

00:03:52.770 --> 00:03:54.660
And the band is named-

00:03:54.660 --> 00:03:58.011
It's a nickname that you had as a kid?

00:03:58.011 --> 00:03:59.592
Yeah, that's what my dad called me

00:03:59.592 --> 00:04:01.380
when I was very young.

00:04:01.380 --> 00:04:03.780
I asked him, "Why'd you call me Weezer?"

00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:07.530
And he said it was after the kid

00:04:07.530 --> 00:04:08.670
in "The Little Rascals,"

00:04:08.670 --> 00:04:10.891
there was a character named Weezer.

00:04:10.891 --> 00:04:11.724
Oh, yeah.

00:04:11.724 --> 00:04:12.632
Was there a Weezer?

00:04:12.632 --> 00:04:14.640
There was like a froggy for a while,

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:16.230
then he probably grew too old

00:04:16.230 --> 00:04:17.160
and they put in Weezer

00:04:17.160 --> 00:04:17.993
or something like that.

00:04:17.993 --> 00:04:19.590
They always needed a weird sounding kid

00:04:19.590 --> 00:04:20.730
or something.

00:04:20.730 --> 00:04:21.630
So I said, "Why him?"

00:04:21.630 --> 00:04:22.463
And he said,

00:04:22.463 --> 00:04:23.970
"Because he's the cool one."

00:04:23.970 --> 00:04:24.870
Oh, that's cool.

00:04:25.770 --> 00:04:27.067
Same reason I had a band called

00:04:27.067 --> 00:04:28.023
"You Little Shit."

00:04:28.023 --> 00:04:31.350
(everyone laughs)

00:04:31.350 --> 00:04:32.343
Thank you, Dad.

00:04:33.743 --> 00:04:36.090
We didn't really go anywhere.

00:04:36.090 --> 00:04:37.650
It's also "My Favorite Little Rascal."

00:04:37.650 --> 00:04:38.557
Yeah.

00:04:38.557 --> 00:04:41.140
(Conan laughs)

